---
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import TypstCompiler from "../components/TypstCompiler.svelte";
---

<PageLayout
  title="Tools"
  description="Generate N-gram model booklets in your browser"
>
  <h1>Tools</h1>

  <p>
    Generate printable materials for <em>LLMs Unplugged</em> activities, matching
    the different ways of working with language models in our lessons. For curated,
    hardbound editions of the same n-gram models, see the
    <a href="/library/">LLMs Unplugged Library</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="booklets-for-dice-generation">Booklets (for dice generation)</h2>

  <p>
    Dice lookup tables for probabilistic text generation. Each entry shows a
    previous-words context, a dice range, and possible next words&mdash;roll the
    dice and read off the next word. Used in the
    <a href="/modules/pretrained-generation/">Pre-trained Model Generation</a> lesson.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Ready-to-print booklets">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/booklets/green-eggs-and-ham.pdf"
          >Green Eggs and Ham</a
        > (Dr Seuss, 5 pages)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/booklets/the-cat-in-the-hat.pdf"
          >The Cat in the Hat</a
        > (Dr Seuss, 7 pages)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/booklets/beatles.pdf">Beatles Lyrics</a> (35 pages)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/booklets/a-christmas-carol.pdf"
          >A Christmas Carol</a
        > (Dickens, 52 pages)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/booklets/frankenstein.pdf">Frankenstein</a>
        (Shelley, 101 pages)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/booklets/collected-hemingway.pdf"
          >Collected Hemingway</a
        > (379 pages)
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="cutouts">Cutouts</h2>

  <p>
    Printable token cards for hands-on training. Cut out the tokens, read the
    previous-word label on each card. Spread them on a table during the <a
      href="/modules/training/">Training</a
    > lesson (cutouts variant) &mdash; or sort them into piles as an optional extension.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Ready-to-print cutouts">
    <p>
      Print these double-sided. They're pre-rotated so the back of each sheet
      lines up with the front for clean cutting under the default <em
        >long-edge</em
      > duplex binding. If your printer comes out misaligned, switch to
      <em>short-edge</em> binding instead.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/cutouts/green-eggs-and-ham.pdf"
          >Green Eggs and Ham</a
        > (Dr Seuss, 36 pages)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/cutouts/where-is-the-green-sheep.pdf"
          >Where Is the Green Sheep?</a
        > (Mem Fox and Judy Horacek, 12 pages)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/cutouts/were-going-on-a-bear-hunt.pdf"
          >We&rsquo;re Going on a Bear Hunt</a
        > (Michael Rosen, 26 pages)
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="search-sheets">Search sheets</h2>

  <p>
    The same matching game as the cutouts, with the cutting taken out. Each
    corpus is shuffled and dealt across one page per participant, so everyone
    searches their own sheet for the word just called out and reads out what
    follows it. Nobody holds the whole model &mdash; it only exists across the
    room &mdash; which makes these a quick way to run <a
      href="/modules/generation/">Generation</a
    > with a large group and no scissors.
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Ready-to-print search sheets">
    <p>
      Print these single-sided and hand out one sheet each. The first page is
      the briefing for whoever is running the activity; every page after it is
      one participant&rsquo;s sheet, and they are all different. How many sheets
      a set holds is set by the length of its text, given in brackets: a set
      only works properly for roughly that many people, because every missing
      sheet takes its share of the model with it. A shorter text makes a smaller
      set.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/sheets/green-eggs-and-ham.pdf"
          >Green Eggs and Ham</a
        > (Dr Seuss, 15 sheets)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/sheets/peter-rabbit.pdf"
          >The Tale of Peter Rabbit</a
        > (Beatrix Potter, 17 sheets)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/sheets/the-cat-in-the-hat.pdf"
          >The Cat in the Hat</a
        > (Dr Seuss, 36 sheets; <a
          href="/assets/pdfs/sheets/the-cat-in-the-hat-2up-a4.pdf"
          >A5 sheets two-up on A4</a
        >)
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/assets/pdfs/sheets/australia.pdf">Australia</a> (Simple English
        Wikipedia, 79 sheets)
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="generate-your-own">Generate your own</h2>

  <p>
    Upload a text file or paste text, choose your options, and download a
    PDF&mdash;no server required. Your text is processed entirely in your
    browser.
  </p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Enter your text</strong>: upload a file or paste text directly
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Add metadata</strong>: provide a title (required) and optionally
      an author
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Choose options</strong>: select the n-gram size and output type
      (booklet or cutouts)
    </li>
    <li><strong>Generate</strong>: preview as SVG or download as PDF</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Supported file formats</h3>

  <ul>
    <li><code>.txt</code> &mdash; plain text files</li>
    <li>
      <code>.md</code> &mdash; markdown files (treated as plain text)
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>.docx</code> &mdash; Microsoft Word documents (text extracted, formatting
      ignored)
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>.pdf</code> &mdash; PDF documents (best-effort text extraction; scanned/image-based
      PDFs won&rsquo;t work)
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Callout type="warning" title="Experimental">
    <p>
      The in-browser compiler and processing modules are downloaded on first
      use, which may take a moment.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <TypstCompiler client:visible />
</PageLayout>
